WEBVTT
NOTE Introduction to Dynamics

1
00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:04.400
In today's video, we are going to be
introduced

2
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.900
to dynamics. First of all, what are
dynamics?

3
00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:11.800
In physics, dynamics are the study of
motion

4
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:15.120
and, more particularly, forces.

5
00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:20.240
The difference between dynamics and
kinematics

6
00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:24.560
is that in kinematics, we study about
motion

7
00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:29.240
itself, while in dynamics, we study how
and

8
00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:33.720
what causes the motion. For example, if
someone

9
00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:36.640
pushes a box, we would be studying the
force

10
00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:40.180
that the person exerts onto this box that
makes

11
00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:47.370
the box move. And this directly correlates

12
00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:53.430
into my next subject about Newton's laws.

13
00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:56.530
So I'll be introducing Newton's three laws
right

14
00:00:56.530 --> 00:00:59.950
now, and these basically govern what we
know

15
00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:04.750
about movement, motion, and dynamics
itself.

16
00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:08.570
First of all, an object that is at rest
stays

17
00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:11.410
at rest and a moving object stays in
motion

18
00:01:11.410 --> 00:01:15.150
until a net external force is acted on it.

19
00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:19.550
So what does that mean? Well, basically,
anything

20
00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:25.130
that has a velocity or is moving will keep
that

21
00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:30.770
velocity until a force is acted upon it.

22
00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:33.550
What does this mean? This means that
forces

23
00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:38.370
are the only thing that can move an
object.

24
00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:41.630
Nothing else can cause an object to move.

25
00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:45.410
And you might be wondering, well, if I
roll

26
00:01:45.410 --> 00:01:47.970
a ball, why does it slow down? Well,
that's

27
00:01:47.970 --> 00:01:50.410
because there's actually a friction force
acting

28
00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:57.770
upon it. There always is a force that
makes

29
00:01:57.770 --> 00:02:02.210
an object do what it does in motion, and
this

30
00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:04.490
friction force has two components. We'll
talk

31
00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:07.930
about that later. Next, the acceleration
of

32
00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:17.210
an object is directly proportional to the

33
00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:23.110
what this is saying is acceleration is the
net

34
00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:27.350
force over mass, inversely proportional to
mass

35
00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:29.970
and directly proportional to net force.

36
00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:34.950
And with this, we can get our first
formula,

37
00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:37.690
probably arguably the most important
formula

38
00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:40.170
in high school physics.

39
00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:45.190
F net equals acceleration times mass or
MA,

40
00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:48.610
and this is a really important formula.

41
00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:52.770
So this basically describes and
demonstrates

42
00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:58.990
how a force can induce movement and
acceleration,

43
00:02:58.990 --> 00:03:03.850
and it's purely based off of its mass and
the

44
00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:08.420
force given. And for every action, there
is

45
00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:11.540
an equal and opposite reaction. This is
also

46
00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:15.340
a really, really important law, and it's
quizzed

47
00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:19.920
a lot of times. And of course, you might
be

48
00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:23.520
very, very familiar with this law, but it
is

49
00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:27.000
also good to remember. If you, let's say,
punch

50
00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:30.780
a block of wood, that wood is going to
exert

51
00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:35.680
the same force as your fist does on your
hand.

52
00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:39.900
If you catch a baseball, let's say, if you
catch

53
00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:43.380
a baseball with your hand, that baseball
is

54
00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:46.380
going to exert the same amount of force as
it

55
00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:49.980
does, as your hand does on the baseball.

56
00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:53.960
And that's basically the third law, and
it's

57
00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:56.340
really useful for a lot of thinking
questions.

58
00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:00.960
And these are very, very important laws.

59
00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:05.380
They basically make up what we know about
dynamics,

60
00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:08.000
and they're the foundation for the
introduction.

61
00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:12.400
We derive most of our formulas from these
laws,

62
00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:14.420
and I'll get into that later.
